{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=check) with a combined new obligation and its first direct check, taking up the escalation the first confirmed defect left open. The obligation c-single-vote-per-term (consensus_safety) states the local half of Raft's election safety as a requirement on the durable vote record: once the record holds (T, C), any later write of the record for term T must be for C, binding both writers of that record. The question is registered with disposition ready_for_check on principal fact f-vote-record (preservation lifecycle), citing b-elect-self and b-vote-grant under A1, with b-heartbeat-fastpath and b-term-observation as support, and bound by bind-raft-elect-self at the electSelf declaration, whose unconditional persistVote is the second writer. The check (plan check-plan-vote.json, harness check-harness-vote.go) fixes the discriminator: over the real NetworkTransport with a socket-free StreamLayer and one held StableStore CurrentTerm write, a stale heartbeat from a deposed leader carrying term 6 is parked while a candidate of term 7 asks for a vote, which the node grants since its published term is still 1; releasing the held write publishes term 6, one below the term just voted in, and the node's own election then derives published+1 = 7 and persists a self-vote over the earlier record. The monitor's admission is the first durable record, its applicability requires the second record's candidate to differ, and the assertion is that the second record's term differs from the first's, so a shared term is judged as the violation itself rather than through a proxy. I verified the exact submitted harness bytes locally in a fresh copy of the captured module: the test starts, exits 0 and emits first_vote_observed with term 7 and candidate d1-pipe (granted, term_after_release 6, state Follower) followed by second_vote_observed with term 7 and candidate f1-pipe, reproducibly across six runs. Counterevidence is explicit: the grant path re-grants a duplicate request for the same candidate without rewriting the record, so only differing candidates are judged; without the delayed write the published term is monotonic and the next election proposes a strictly greater term; the witness is the local record only, while two leaders in one term would additionally need other voters' records corrupted; and the reading that only the grant path owes the rule is retained as an alternative. The sourced conflict between the two record-writing paths is recorded in the claim and plan grounding, so it will arrive as an applicability issue to resolve in the correspondence review, and the limits (local scope, election-timer timing, single node and prefix, fixed identity labels, no leader elected and no log replicated) are retained in the plan. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true; the target has not been executed for this product yet, so the next step is the correspondence review of the resulting measured comparison.\"}"}}
